WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.800
The letters T and D occur frequently throughout the Tamil texts in the accompanying book.

00:04.800 --> 00:09.200
It is important not to pronounce these in the same way as English T and D.

00:09.200 --> 00:14.200
Used for Tamil, these letters represent dental sounds, as in French.

00:14.200 --> 00:19.200
When you articulate them, make sure that the tip of your tongue touches the upper front teeth.

00:19.200 --> 00:25.800
Careful listening will show that D has a different pronunciation depending on what other sounds come next to it.

00:25.800 --> 00:33.400
At the beginning of a word, and after N in the middle of a word, it has a sound very close to that of a French D, as just mentioned.

00:33.400 --> 00:41.000
When it occurs between vowels in the middle of a word, however, it sounds more like the "th" in English "other".

00:41.000 --> 00:46.500
Listen carefully to these features of the following words, and then repeat them.

00:46.500 --> 00:48.500
Street

00:48.500 --> 00:50.500
தெரு (teru)

00:50.500 --> 00:53.000
How many

00:53.000 --> 00:55.500
எத்தனை (ettane)

00:55.500 --> 00:58.000
Daily

00:58.000 --> 01:00.500
தினம் (dinam)

01:00.500 --> 01:03.000
That

01:03.000 --> 01:05.500
அந்த (anda)

01:05.500 --> 01:07.500
It

01:07.500 --> 01:10.000
அது (adu)